--TEST--
`Core\Cli::arguments` answers the program's own words in the order they were written, one element per word and no splitting — spec § 15, ADR 0086 § 6
--ARGS--
greet
ada lovelace
--dry-run
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Spec § 15's raw list: what a program reads when it declares no `#[Command]`
// and parses its own line. Three words that between them cover what a shell
// would have done and this format does not: a plain one, one holding a space,
// and one spelled like an option the runner itself might have taken.
array<tainted string> $words = Core\Cli::arguments();
echo Core\Arr::count($words), "\n";

// Order is the shell's, and a word is one element whatever it contains -- the
// bracket around each is what shows the space did not split "ada lovelace"
// into two. Echoing a `tainted` element needs no launderer because ADR 0086
// § 1 makes the terminal a substituting sink rather than a refusing one.
foreach ($words as tainted string $word) {
    echo "[", $word, "]\n";
}
--EXPECT--
3
[greet]
[ada lovelace]
[--dry-run]
